{
  "concept": "A parabola is the set of all points equidistant from a fixed point (the focus) and a fixed line (the directrix), with the vertex, axis of symmetry, and latus rectum naming the key parts of that construction.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "thin black x-axis with arrowheads at both ends, labeled 'x' at right",
    "thin black y-axis with arrowheads at both ends, labeled 'y' at top",
    "heavy dark-blue upward-opening parabola with arrowheads on both branches",
    "filled dark-blue vertex dot at the lowest point of the curve, above the x-axis",
    "black leader line from the vertex dot to the label 'Vertex' on the left",
    "dashed orange vertical line through the vertex spanning the figure height",
    "orange label 'Axis of symmetry' at the upper right of that dashed vertical line",
    "small teal focus dot on the axis of symmetry, above the vertex",
    "teal label 'Focus' to the right of the focus dot",
    "short dashed light-blue horizontal segment through the focus, spanning from one branch to the other (latus rectum)",
    "curved black arrow from the label 'Latus rectum' (upper left, blue text) to the dashed light-blue segment",
    "dashed red horizontal line below the vertex spanning the frame width (directrix)",
    "red label 'Directrix' at the right end of the dashed red line",
    "construction-view only: movable sample point P on the parabola",
    "construction-view only: teal segment from P to the focus labeled with its length",
    "construction-view only: red perpendicular segment from P straight down to the directrix, with a small right-angle square at the foot",
    "construction-view only: matching tick marks / 'equal' annotation showing the two segments have the same length",
    "construction-view only: short vertical segments of length p from vertex to focus and from vertex to directrix, both labeled 'p'"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "focalDistance",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Focus–vertex distance p",
      "range": [0.3, 3, 0.1],
      "default": 1,
      "teaches": "The single parameter p sets the shape: as the focus rises and the directrix drops by the same amount, the parabola opens wider; as p shrinks, the curve tightens around the axis. The vertex always stays halfway between focus and directrix, and the latus rectum is always 4p wide."
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Labeled features", "Distance construction"],
      "default": "Labeled features",
      "teaches": "The vocabulary diagram and the defining property are the same picture: the named parts exist because every point on the curve is equally far from the focus and the directrix."
    },
    {
      "id": "samplePoint",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Point P along the parabola",
      "range": [-3, 3, 0.1],
      "default": 1.6,
      "teaches": "Sliding P anywhere on the curve keeps the two distances equal — the equality is not a coincidence at one spot but the rule that generates every point of the parabola, including the vertex where both distances equal p."
    },
    {
      "id": "featureHover",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "Each vocabulary term — vertex, focus, directrix, axis of symmetry, latus rectum — attaches to one specific object, and hovering isolates it with its own construction lines instead of reading five labels at once."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The named parts",
      "narration": "Start with the standard labeled parabola: a dark blue curve, its vertex, the teal focus above it, the red directrix below, the orange axis of symmetry through both, and the dashed latus rectum across the focus. Hover any feature to light it up alone.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Labeled features", "focalDistance": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why equidistant?",
      "narration": "Switch to the distance construction. A point P on the curve grows a teal segment to the focus and a red perpendicular segment down to the directrix — and they are exactly the same length.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Distance construction", "focalDistance": 1, "samplePoint": 1.6 }
    },
    {
      "title": "It holds everywhere",
      "narration": "Slide P along the curve. The two segments change length together but never differ — this equality is the definition of the parabola, not a special case.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Distance construction", "focalDistance": 1, "samplePoint": -2.4 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The vertex is the midpoint",
      "narration": "Move P to the bottom. Both distances shrink to the same value p: the vertex is the one point sitting halfway between focus and directrix, on the axis of symmetry.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Distance construction", "focalDistance": 1, "samplePoint": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Changing p reshapes the curve",
      "narration": "Increase p: the focus climbs, the directrix drops the same amount, and the parabola opens out wide with a latus rectum of width 4p.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Distance construction", "focalDistance": 2.5, "samplePoint": 1.6 }
    },
    {
      "title": "A tight parabola",
      "narration": "Shrink p and the focus and directrix squeeze toward the vertex, pinching the curve into a narrow channel — same definition, different single parameter.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Labeled features", "focalDistance": 0.4, "samplePoint": 1.6 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Every point on a parabola is the same distance from the focus as from the directrix — the vertex is simply the point where that shared distance is smallest, which is why it sits exactly halfway between them.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Cartesian plane with thin black arrowed axes, origin low and left of center; 'x' at the right end of the horizontal axis, 'y' at the top of the vertical axis. A heavy dark-blue (#1f4e79-ish) upward-opening parabola fills the upper portion with arrowheads on both branches. Its vertex is a filled dark-blue dot sitting noticeably above the x-axis, with a short black leader line to a left-side label 'Vertex'. A dashed vertical orange line passes through the vertex from top to bottom of the frame, labeled 'Axis of symmetry' in orange near the top right of the line. A small teal dot on that line a short way above the vertex is labeled 'Focus' in teal to its right. A dashed light-blue horizontal chord through the focus stretches from one branch to the other; a curved black arrow points to it from the blue label 'Latus rectum' at the upper left. Below the vertex, a dashed red horizontal line spans the full frame width and is labeled 'Directrix' in red at its right end, positioned between the vertex and the x-axis. Keep vertex-to-focus and vertex-to-directrix gaps visually identical. In the 'Distance construction' view, dim the text labels, place a highlighted point P on one branch, draw a teal straight segment P→focus and a red vertical segment P→directrix with a small right-angle marker at the directrix, mark both with equal tick marks and an '=' callout, and show small 'p' brackets between vertex-focus and vertex-directrix. Hover should brighten the chosen feature and gray the others."
}

